digraph ReasoningTree {
  rankdir="TB";
  "N10" [label="(0,0,0) Common pattern: Current LCS-related AI models were developed with relatively small single-modality datasets for individual tasks Source: (12,0,0)"];
  "N12" [label="(0,0,0) Case: Training schemes of current lung cancer risk models require costly bounding box annotations Source: (13,0,0)"];
  "N13" [label="(0,0,0) Common pattern: Costly annotations make building large-scale training datasets infeasible Source: (13,0,0)"];
  "N15" [label="(0,0,0) Phenomenon: Foundation models (FMs) have shown previously unseen abilities to understand diverse data types and execute many tasks in unified fashion Source: (14,0,0)"];
  "N16" [label="(0,0,0) Case: Large FMs updated state-of-the-art performance across NLP, computer vision, and multimodal understanding Source: (15,0,0)"];
  "N17" [label="(0,0,0) Common pattern: FMs demonstrate superior performance across diverse domains through unified learning Source: (15,0,0)"];
  "N18" [label="(0,0,0) Induction: FMs show unified task execution AND achieve SoTA across domains, therefore FMs represent a paradigm shift for handling diverse tasks with multimodal data Source: (0,0,0)"];
  "N20" [label="(0,0,0) Background knowledge: If FMs can handle diverse tasks in general domains, then medical FMs could address multitask medical workflows Source: (0,0,0)"];
  "N24" [label="(0,0,0) Case: High bar exists to systematically curate large-scale multimodal multitask datasets from real-world LCS workflow Source: (19,0,0)"];
  "N25" [label="(0,0,0) Case: Extensive domain expertise required to query, preprocess, and align 3D medical images with diverse clinical data for LCS tasks Source: (20,0,0)"];
  "N26" [label="(0,0,0) Common pattern: Data curation for LCS requires systematic integration of high-dimensional 3D images with heterogeneous clinical data across multiple tasks Source: (0,0,0)"];
  "N27" [label="(0,0,0) Induction: High bar for dataset curation AND extensive expertise needed for alignment, therefore data curation is a major technical barrier for LCS foundation models Source: (0,0,0)"];
  "N28" [label="(0,0,0) Case: No scalable and adaptable FM exists dedicated to interpreting multimodal LCS data, especially 3D CT at different scales, and performing diverse LCS tasks Source: (21,0,0)"];
  "N29" [label="(0,0,0) Case: Due to high dimensionality of volumetric CT, existing efforts only used small 2D/3D CNNs and small vision Transformers at affordable computational costs Source: (22,0,0)"];
  "N30" [label="(0,0,0) Common pattern: Computational constraints limit model scale for 3D medical imaging Source: (22,0,0)"];
  "N31" [label="(0,0,0) Induction: No scalable LCS FM exists AND computational constraints limit 3D model scale, therefore model architecture scalability is a major technical barrier Source: (0,0,0)"];
  "N35" [label="(0,0,0) Phenomenon: An integrated and scalable data curation approach can align high-dimensional medical images with clinical datasets for 17 LCS workflow tasks and 49 data elements Source: (23,0,0)"];
  "N36" [label="(0,0,0) Background knowledge: If a model perceives multimodal data (3D CT volumes and clinical data) and performs multiple LCS workflow tasks, then it addresses the multitask LCS challenge Source: (0,0,0)"];
  "N38" [label="(0,0,0) Case: M3FM architecture consists of CTViT, Text Transformer, Task Encoder, and Predictors for handling multimodal inputs Source: (28,0,0)"];
  "N41" [label="(0,0,0) Common pattern: M3FM uses specialized components for different modalities and unified multitask learning strategy Source: (0,0,0)"];
  "N43" [label="(0,0,0) Phenomenon: Extensive experiments show M3FM outperforms previous SoTA through large-scale multimodal and multitask learning, with ability to identify informative data elements and adapt to out-of-distribution tasks Source: (26,0,0)"];
  "N44" [label="(0,0,0) Background knowledge: If a model outperforms SoTA through large-scale learning and shows adaptability, then it demonstrates superior generalization capability Source: (0,0,0)"];
  "N46" [label="(0,0,0) Deductive rule: If integrated data curation addresses data barriers AND scalable architecture addresses model barriers AND experiments show superior performance, then the combined approach successfully enables LCS foundation model Source: (0,0,0)"];
  "N6" [label="(0,0,0) Phenomenon: LCS involves multiple clinical tasks including nodule detection/characterization, Lung-RADS management, risk estimation, and incidental findings diagnosis Source: (7,0,0)"];
  "N7" [label="(0,0,0) Background knowledge: If AI can process multimodal data and perform multiple tasks, then it can improve quality and efficiency of complex medical workflows Source: (0,0,0)"];
  "N9" [label="(0,0,0) Case: Deep learning methods achieved promising results for individual LCS tasks (e.g., lung cancer detection/risk estimation with LDCT, CVD risk prediction) Source: (8,0,0)"];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) Develop and evaluate a scalable data-curation pipeline and a Medical Multimodal Multitask Foundation Model for lung-cancer screening that jointly uses 3D CT imaging and clinical text/data representations to support diverse tasks such as nodule detection, Lung-RADS assessment, cancer risk prediction, and cardiovascular risk prediction, aiming to improve on existing task-specific models within a unified multimodal multitask framework.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) When LCS workflow challenges, limitations of existing task-specific AI, foundation-model capabilities, data-curation barriers, scalable multimodal architecture, and performance evidence are considered together, they motivate a unified LCS foundation-model research program."];
  "R1" [label="(0,0,0) Existing LCS-related AI work includes promising deep-learning results on individual tasks, but is characterized by individual-task development on relatively small single-modality datasets."];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) Because LCS involves multiple clinical tasks, AI systems capable of performing multiple tasks could help improve the quality and efficiency of LCS workflows."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) The broad success of foundation models in unified task execution motivates exploring medical foundation models for multitask medical workflows such as LCS."];
  "R14" [label="(0,0,0) A scalable data-curation approach that aligns high-dimensional medical images with clinical datasets for 17 LCS workflow tasks can support multimodal multitask LCS modeling."];
  "R15" [label="(0,0,0) Together, the integrated LCS data-curation support, the M3FM multimodal multitask architecture, and the reported performance/adaptability evidence support a combined LCS foundation-model approach."];
  "R2" [label="(0,0,0) Because current lung cancer risk model training schemes require costly bounding-box annotations, such annotation requirements can hinder scaling training datasets."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) M3FM combines modality-specific components for multimodal inputs with joint multimodal and multitask training."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) M3FM's reported state-of-the-art performance and adaptability are consistent with improved generalization capability under its large-scale multimodal multitask learning approach."];
